idea per paragraph.\nWhen the file changed after you wrote it, re-read it: the user's edits are your next instruction.","generic":"When you produce a plan, spec or report longer than ~50 lines, write it to a .md file and open it for the user in Mark.\nMark renders rich Markdown. Use the right tool for each part instead of plain prose:\n- Diagrams — ```mermaid fences; pick the type that fits: flowchart (processes, decisions), sequenceDiagram (who calls whom), classDiagram, stateDiagram-v2 (lifecycles), erDiagram (data models), gantt (schedules), timeline (history, milestones), gitGraph (branches), mindmap (idea maps), journey (user journeys), quadrantChart (priorities), pie, xychart-beta (bar/line charts), sankey-beta (flows of quantities), block-beta, packet-beta, kanban, architecture-beta, radar-beta, treemap-beta, requirementDiagram, C4Context. Prefer a diagram to a paragraph describing a structure.\n- Charts from data — ```vega-lite with a JSON spec; quick flowcharts — ```flowchart (flowchart.js); quick sequences — ```sequence.\n- Tables (with column alignment) for comparisons, options and matrices.\n- Callouts for what must not be missed: > [!NOTE], [!TIP], [!IMPORTANT], [!WARNING], [!CAUTION]; also info, todo, success, question, failure, danger, bug, example, quote, abstract. Put a title after the tag; add - or + to make it foldable.\n- Task lists (- [ ] / - [x]) for next steps and checklists.\n- Math — $…$ inline and $$…$$ blocks (KaTeX).\n- Code — fenced blocks with a language for highlighting.\n- Footnotes ([^1]) for sources and asides; YAML front matter for metadata; emoji :shortcodes:; links and images.\n\nKeep it scannable: short sections with headings, one idea per paragraph.\nWhen the file changed after you wrote it, re-read it: the user's edits are your next instruction."}},"common":{"cancel":"Cancel","ok":"OK","close":"Close"},"dialog":{"cancel":"Cancel","changesWillBeLost":"Changes will be lost","close":"Close","dontSave":"Don't Save","file":"File","